[38;2;124;124;124m**M = ∂D**: Since $\rho$ is continuous, $\partial D = \{x : |x| = \rho(x/|x|)\} [0m[38;2;124;124;124m= \{\rho(u) u : u \in S^{n-1}\}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**M is parameterized by $\Phi(u) = \rho(u) u$**: This is a homeomorphism from [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$S^{n-1}$ to M (with inverse $x \mapsto x/|x|$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Smoothness of M ↔ differentiability of $\rho$**: At any point $u_0 \in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mS^{n-1}$ that is not a coordinate singularity (i.e., not $(\pm 1, 0, \ldots, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m0)$), using local coordinates $(s_1, \ldots, s_{n-1})$ on $S^{n-1}$, the surface[0m[38;2;124;124;124mM is parameterized by $\Phi(s) = \rho(s) \cdot u(s)$. The partial derivative is:[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$$\frac{\partial \Phi}{\partial s_i} = \frac{\partial \rho}{\partial s_i} u + [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\rho \frac{\partial u}{\partial s_i}$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis exists if and only if $\frac{\partial \rho}{\partial s_i}$ exists. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mMoreover, the vectors $\frac{\partial \Phi}{\partial s_i}$ are linearly [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mindependent (since $\rho > 0$ and $\frac{\partial u}{\partial s_i}$ are linearly[0m[38;2;124;124;124mindependent tangent vectors to $S^{n-1}$, and the $u$ component is normal to the[0m[38;2;124;124;124msphere). So M is a $C^1$ submanifold at $\Phi(u_0)$ if and only if $\rho$ is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$C^1$ at $u_0$.[0m

