[38;2;124;124;124mW(u_1)$ is not differentiable at any $u_0$ where $u_1$ is not at a local [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mextremum on $S^{n-1}$. The set where $u_1$ is at a local extremum is $\{(\pm 1, [0m[38;2;124;124;124m0, \ldots, 0)\}$, which has $(n-1)$-dimensional measure zero on $S^{n-1}$ (it's [0m[38;2;124;124;124mjust 2 points). At every other point $u_0$, there exists a tangent direction $v$[0m[38;2;124;124;124mto $S^{n-1}$ with $\nabla_{S^{n-1}} u_1 \cdot v \ne 0$, and since $W$ is nowhere[0m[38;2;124;124;124mdifferentiable, $f$ (hence $\rho$) is not differentiable at $u_0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Conclusion**: M is not $C^1$ (hence not smooth) at any point $\Phi(u_0)$ where[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$u_0 \ne (\pm 1, 0, \ldots, 0)$. The set of non-smooth points of M is $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\Phi(S^{n-1} \setminus \{(\pm 1, 0, \ldots, 0)\})$, which has full $(n-1)$-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdimensional Hausdorff measure in M (since $\Phi$ is a bi-Lipschitz [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mhomeomorphism, being a continuous map on a compact set with continuous inverse).[0m[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore, M is not smooth almost everywhere.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis counterexample shows that the answer is **NO**: a starlike surface need not[0m[38;2;124;124;124mbe smooth almost everywhere.[0m

